      {
        "id": 3494983,
        "postDate": "2026-07-11T07:50:56.430Z",
        "rawMarkdown": "I personally believe this is a fault in the imaging system causing repeated frames or dropped frames. Zebrafish embryo development is a continuous dynamic process. From the cleavage stage to the gastrulation stage, cells are constantly undergoing division, migration, and tissue deformation. Even in slower developmental phases, there are still sub-pixel-level cell displacements and fluctuations in signal intensity/grayscale. It is impossible for the entire 3D volume to show completely zero change, let alone produce perfectly repeated frames where subtraction yields exactly 0.",
        "content": "<p>I personally believe this is a fault in the imaging system causing repeated frames or dropped frames. Zebrafish embryo development is a continuous dynamic process. From the cleavage stage to the gastrulation stage, cells are constantly undergoing division, migration, and tissue deformation. Even in slower developmental phases, there are still sub-pixel-level cell displacements and fluctuations in signal intensity/grayscale. It is impossible for the entire 3D volume to show completely zero change, let alone produce perfectly repeated frames where subtraction yields exactly 0.</p>",
        "parent": {},
